1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
In this episode we get HarnessStation running on Windows — including the security warning that every single viewer is going to run into, and why it's nothing to worry about.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,867
The whole flow is four steps: download the installer, run it, get past Windows SmartScreen, and launch.

3
00:00:12,867 --> 00:00:14,000
Let's do it live.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,167
First, grab the Windows installer from the releases page.

5
00:00:17,167 --> 00:00:22,000
It's a normal Windows installer — download it and let it land in your Downloads folder.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,597
Double-click the installer to run it.

7
00:00:25,597 --> 00:00:29,000
And this is where Windows steps in.

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:31,027
You'll see this blue box: “Windows protected your PC.

9
00:00:31,027 --> 00:00:32,290
” Don't panic — this is expected.

10
00:00:32,290 --> 00:00:34,202
It shows up because the app isn't code-signed yet.

11
00:00:34,202 --> 00:00:40,781
Code signing is a paid certificate that tells Windows who published the software; this project doesn't have one yet, so Windows shows the warning for any unknown publisher.

12
00:00:40,781 --> 00:00:43,000
It is not a sign that anything is wrong with the download.

13
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,042
To continue, click “More info,” and then “Run anyway.

14
00:00:47,042 --> 00:00:52,000
” The installer proceeds normally from here — next, next, finish.

15
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,034
You do not need to install any extra runtime.

16
00:00:54,034 --> 00:01:00,000
HarnessStation uses WebView2 for its interface, and that already ships with Windows 10 and 11 — so there's nothing else to download.

17
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,889
Launch it, and you're in.

18
00:01:03,889 --> 00:01:07,000
That's Windows done.

19
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:07,714
One more thing.

20
00:01:07,714 --> 00:01:14,476
If you'd rather not run unsigned software at all, you don't have to — you can build the app yourself from source and inspect every line first.

21
00:01:14,476 --> 00:01:16,000
We cover that path in Episode 4.

22
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,391
Next up: the same thing on Linux — AppImage or dot-deb, plus the one dependency that trips people up.

23
00:01:20,391 --> 00:01:21,000
See you there.

